  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Overview
    
    A pressure-binding technique of orca origin that forces the Berg's deep ice-memories — accumulated impressions of pressure, cold, and geological weight — into carved crystalline or bone vessels, weaponizing them on impact. Targets do not merely suffer physical trauma; they experience compressed centuries of crushing depth in a single moment. The technique draws from the third school of Berg magic: pressure-depth, the domain of blood, force, and communion with the deep.
    
    ## Origin and Mechanism
    
    The Wake-Singers, the orca shaman-cult guided by Grandmother Deep, developed Rumzunkaidkak in volcanic laboratories — reportedly by accident, while testing whether the Berg's ancient ice could be made to convey its memories in reverse. It can. Ritualists carve spiraling channels into ice-core or captured bone, then bleed into the channels while chanting the names of the drowned, binding the Berg's oldest impressions into the vessel.
    
    The cost is not trivial. Each use burns a memory from the practitioner's own past. Minor losses — a pod-mate's face, a childhood hunting ground — are common. Catastrophic losses — the reason one joined the hunt, the name of one's dead — are documented.
    
    ## Proliferation
    
    Keeper Hollows, the hermit-miner who camps at the sealed Drowned Ledge entrance, learned the technique through means the record does not clarify and sold it to anyone willing to pay in blood. The Icebound Exchange, Aurora Stack's dominant merchant guild, now manufactures crude versions. Their work is inconsistent: targets sometimes survive, left with years of pressure-memory that never resolves. The Aureate merchant faction and Light-Regent Xouhoulong himself are documented practitioners — Xouhoulong's secret authorization of Wake-Singer research being among the revelations that ended his regency.
    
    ## Effects and Legacy
    
    Rumzunkaidkak served as catalyst in the Vum∴tenebra and in Thanksgiving, the latter reportedly ending only when the cycle of its use was broken. Sustained application produces an unsettling secondary effect: the carved spirals self-extend, the ice remembering the pressure-song and carving new channels autonomously. Defensive imprints left behind have been observed to calcify into structures that pulse with a rhythm resembling a damaged heartbeat, persisting long after the ritualists have departed.
    
    The technique was created during the Faction Wars. It has outlasted them comfortably.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
